<LessonLayout title="Catechist's Guide — Anointing of the Sick" cic="CCC 1499-1532" scripture="James 5:14-15; Mark 6:12-13">

  <section class="content">
    <h2>1. Class Summary</h2>
    <p><strong>Duration:</strong> 60 minutes</p>
    <p><strong>Central Theme:</strong> The Anointing of the Sick as a sacrament of spiritual healing and divine comfort in times of frailty.</p>
    <p><strong>Objectives:</strong></p>
    <ul>
      <li>Demystify "Extreme Unction" as a sacrament only for imminent death.</li>
      <li>Explain the biblical foundations in James and the Gospels.</li>
      <li>Transmit the effects of peace, strength, and forgiveness granted by the Holy Spirit.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="content">
    <h2>2. Suggested Structure</h2>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Section</th>
          <th>Duration</th>
          <th>Suggested Activity</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Opening</td>
          <td>10 min</td>
          <td>Present the Big Question and the story of Job.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Doctrine</td>
          <td>20 min</td>
          <td>Explain the institution (James 5) and who can receive it.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Deepening</td>
          <td>15 min</td>
          <td>Work through the 4 effects of the sacrament with real-life examples.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Reflection</td>
          <td>10 min</td>
          <td>Space for workbook questions and sharing experiences.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Closing</td>
          <td>5 min</td>
          <td>Catechist's prayer and sending forth.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </section>

  <section class="content">
    <h2>3. Notes for the Catechist</h2>
    <div class="note-box">
      <p><strong>Sensitivity:</strong> Treat this topic with great delicacy. Some students likely have sick relatives or have recently lost someone. Focus on comfort, not fear.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="note-box">
      <p><strong>Distinction:</strong> Ensure you distinguish between physical healing (a miracle) and spiritual healing (a sacrament). God can grant both, but the sacrament always grants the second.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="note-box">
      <p><strong>Matter and Form:</strong> Remember to mention that the matter is blessed olive oil and the form is the priest's words while anointing the forehead and hands.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="note-box">
      <p><strong>Priests Only:</strong> It is vital to clarify that only bishops and presbyters (priests) can administer this sacrament, as it is linked to the forgiveness of sins.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="content">
    <h2>4. Age Adaptation</h2>
    <p><strong>🌱 Seed (Children):</strong> Focus on Jesus as the friend who takes care of us when we are in pain. Use the example of oil as a caress from God.</p>
    <p><strong>🌿 Sprouts (Youth):</strong> Speak about the meaning of suffering and how Anointing helps us not to feel alone during life's trials.</p>
    <p><strong>🪴 Root (Adults):</strong> Explain the importance of not delaying the sacrament until the last moment. Encourage filial trust.</p>
    <p><strong>🌳 Tree (Formation):</strong> Deepen the understanding of the mystery of Redemption through pain and the importance of Viaticum for the soul.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="content">
    <h2>5. Supplementary Resources</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>YouCat:</strong> Questions 243-247 on Anointing.</li>
      <li><strong>Catechism:</strong> Points 1499-1532 for deep reading.</li>
      <li><strong>Recommended Video:</strong> "The Sacrament of Anointing" from Catholic.net or similar on YouTube.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="content">
    <h2>6. Difficult Questions</h2>
    <p><strong>Why did my grandfather die anyway if he received the Anointing?</strong> The sacrament is not an insurance policy against physical death, but a grace so that the soul is strong. Sometimes healing means bringing the person to God's presence in peace.</p>
    <p><strong>Can a deacon give the Anointing?</strong> No, only a priest. This is because the sacrament can include the forgiveness of sins, something reserved for the presbyteral order.</p>
    <p><strong>What if the person is unconscious?</strong> The priest can administer it if it is presumed that the sick person, if conscious, would have desired it.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="content">
    <h2>7. Catechist's Prayer</h2>
    <blockquote>
      <p>
        Lord, make me an instrument of Your comfort.<br />
        May my words not be empty theories about pain,<br />
        but an echo of Your closeness to those who suffer.<br />
        Give me the wisdom to speak of Anointing<br />
        as the gift of hope that it truly is.<br />
        Amen.
      </p>
    </blockquote>
  </section>

